WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.060
Welcome to Grade 5 English, Unit 6: A Different Type of Story.

2
00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:09.560
This unit explores the different
types of story writers create.

3
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:17.280
You will meet five genres on the library shelves —
adventure, mystery, fable, legend and realistic fiction —

4
00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:20.020
and learn the features that tell them apart.

5
00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:27.307
A courtroom story then shows realistic fiction at work,
while twenty-five words built from the prefixes un-,

6
00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:33.300
dis- and in- and the endings -ible,
-able and -ous stretch your vocabulary.

7
00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:39.300
Alongside this you will learn to link ideas
smoothly with conjunctions and connective adverbs,

8
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.120
and use them to write your own story opening.

9
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:47.920
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

10
00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:50.320
By the end of this unit,

11
00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:57.260
you will be able to identify and compare the five story
genres in this unit and describe the features of each.

12
00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:04.740
You will also support inferences about characters,
viewpoint and language choices with evidence from the text.

13
00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:12.010
You will also work out the meaning of longer words
from the prefixes un-, dis-, in- and the endings -ible,

14
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:13.860
-able and -ous.

15
00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.360
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

16
00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:25.420
Your vocabulary work begins with these words:
cemetery, secretary, category, variety, symbol, albeit.

17
00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:30.920
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

18
00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:34.520
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

19
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:41.620
The reading sequence includes The Library Tour:
Adventure and Mystery and The Library Tour: Fable,

20
00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:43.500
Legend and Realistic Fiction.

21
00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:45.400
Read actively.

22
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:50.420
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

23
00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:54.800
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

24
00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:59.480
The first language focus is What
Are Connectives and Conjunctions?

25
00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:03.760
A connective is any word or phrase
that links ideas across your writing.

26
00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:10.540
A conjunction is one kind of connective, and it joins
words, phrases or clauses inside a single sentence.

27
00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:16.840
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

28
00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:23.180
In speaking, you will complete
Speaking 1: Genre Detective Game.

29
00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:28.240
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

30
00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.960
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

31
00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:35.620
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

32
00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:37.340
You are ready to begin.

33
00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:43.960
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

34
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.620
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

35
00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:53.900
Use the AI tutor for hints and feedback any time, and
bring your questions to a live session too if you join one.
